	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The primary outcome was development of any de novo malignancy after lung transplantation among recipients without a documented pretransplant cancer history. Gradient Boosting achieved the highest held-out test AUC of 0.746 for overall malignancy prediction. Confidence intervals and exact test-set accuracy, precision, sensitivity, specificity, NPV, and F1-score were not reported in the main article. Secondary models achieved AUC 0.642 for skin versus non-skin malignancy using Gradient Boosting and AUC 0.726 with 82.6% accuracy for BCC versus SCC using Random Forest. Formal calibration analysis and DCA were not reported.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Of 38,788 recipients without a prior malignancy, only 30,917 remained after linkage to malignancy follow-up data and exclusion of individuals lost to follow-up. The number and characteristics of excluded patients were not reported, creating risk of informative selection, particularly because adequate follow-up is directly related to malignancy ascertainment.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The models included post-transplant variables such as hospital length of stay, ECMO_72HOURS, and treatment for rejection within 1 year. The authors acknowledge that these predictors “may introduce temporal misalignment” and limit use for early or pretransplant prediction, creating temporal leakage and reverse-causation risk.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: De novo malignancy was identified from longitudinal malignancy follow-up records, but no fixed prediction horizon or minimum follow-up duration was defined. Follow-up time differed substantially between patients with and without malignancy, and the analysis treated malignancy as a binary outcome without accounting for censoring or competing mortality, creating potential outcome misclassification.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study used a random internal split without external or temporal validation. It is not stated that variable screening, correlation assessment, and all preprocessing were restricted to the training subset. Confidence intervals, calibration, and decision-curve analysis were absent, and high-dimensional screening involved hundreds of candidate predictors. The authors explicitly acknowledge that generalizability is limited by the lack of external validation.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates machine-learning prediction of post-transplant malignancy and skin-cancer subtypes in lung transplant recipients using a national thoracic-transplant registry. It is directly applicable to AI research in thoracic organ transplantation, although the inclusion of postoperative predictors limits applicability to early risk prediction.

